<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Gojoseon</h1><p class="st-meta">700 BCE – 108 BCE (approx.) · capital Wanggeom-seong</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The first Korean kingdom known from written sources, centred on the Liaodong and northern Korean region until its conquest by Han China in 108 BCE.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Korean tradition credits its founding to the mythical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dangun">Dangun</a> in 2333 BCE, but the historical state appears in Chinese sources from about the 7th century BCE as a power of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao</a> river basin and northwestern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, linked to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaoning">Liaoning</a> bronze dagger culture. Its centre later shifted to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taedong-river">Taedong</a> river near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyongyang">Pyongyang</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Early rulers took the title of king by the 4th century BCE and clashed with the Chinese state of Yan. Around 194 BCE the refugee Wiman ousted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jun-of-gojoseon">King Jun</a> and founded a dynasty that prospered as a middleman between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han China</a> and the southern Korean polities. A short law code of eight articles, three of which survive, punished murder, injury and theft.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ugeo-of-gojoseon">King Ugeo's</a> control of trade and refusal to submit provoked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-han">Emperor Wu of Han</a>, whose armies besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wanggeom-seong">Wanggeom-seong</a>; the city fell in 108 BCE after Ugeo's assassination. Han set up four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commandery">commanderies</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lelang-commandery">Lelang</a>, and later Korean states looked back to Gojoseon as the founding Korean polity.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/gojoseon.jpg" alt="Gojoseon" width="320" height="501"><figcaption>Image: Asieon · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Old_Chos%C5%8Fn.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>700 BCE – 108 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Wanggeom-seong</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Jun of Gojoseon, Wiman, Ugeo</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Indigenous shamanistic beliefs</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/north-korea">North Korea</a>, <a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Old Joseon, Wiman Joseon, Gija Joseon</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-404&amp;focus=gojoseon" aria-label="Gojoseon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7795 779 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Gojoseon lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8115 1035L7769 1191"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7769 1191h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8115 1035h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8115 1035h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=-404&amp;focus=gojoseon">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
